(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 42-year-old man has mild plaque psoriasis affecting the elbows and knees. He has not used any treatment. What is the most appropriate first-line approach?","choices":["A tapering course of oral prednisone","A biologic agent","Salicylic acid and coal tar","Methotrexate immediately","Emollients, topical steroids, calcipotriene"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. First-line treatment of mild plaque psoriasis is emollients, topical steroids, and calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue with the quickest action, with phototherapy using ultraviolet B best for smaller stubborn areas. Salicylic acid and coal tar are second line. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 26-year-old Black man reports tender bumps on his neck and jawline that appear after shaving. Examination shows erythematous papules, several with a visible central hair shaft curving back into the skin. What is the underlying process?","choices":["Follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess sebum","An allergic reaction to shaving products","Follicular infection by Staphylococcus aureus","A foreign body reaction to re-entrant hair","Apocrine gland occlusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pseudofolliculitis barbae is a foreign body reaction to hair in any shaved area. The cut hair curves into the follicular wall and penetrates the skin to form a tender red papule or pustule, and it occurs commonly in Black men because of tightly curled facial hair together with keratin gene variations. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with non-purulent cellulitis of the leg is started on cephalexin. Forty-eight hours later he still has fever and the affected area has extended. What should be considered?","choices":["Necrotizing fasciitis","A normal first-day treatment response","Allergy to cephalexin","Deep vein thrombosis alone","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The differential specifically states that necrotizing fasciitis should be considered if there is no response to antibiotics within 48 hours. It may initially be diagnosed as cellulitis, with the patient sent home and returning worse. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 44-year-old runner has a painful lesion on the plantar surface of the forefoot. Pressing straight down on it reproduces the pain, whereas squeezing it from the sides does not. The skin lines run through the lesion, and paring it reveals a central cone of hard keratin. What is the diagnosis?","choices":["Clavus (corn)","Verruca vulgaris","Plantar fibroma","Callus","Epidermoid cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A clavus results from pressure on a localised area, creating a cone-shaped central core of hard keratin. Pain occurs on direct downward pressure, and the skin lines run through the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, pruritus, and skin discoloration. Examination demonstrates poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting edema, and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. What is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Order patch testing","Begin oral cephalexin","Obtain a bacterial wound culture","Perform a skin biopsy","Perform an ankle-brachial index"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management only once adequate arterial circulation has been established. An ankle-brachial index or toe pressure should be obtained when peripheral arterial disease is suspected — as the diminished pulses here suggest — before initiating substantial compression. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy with neurofibromatosis type 1 has a large, diffuse soft tissue mass running along a nerve in the thigh. What is a plexiform neurofibroma, and what is the concern with it?","choices":["A tumour confined to brain and spinal cord","Freckles under 5 mm in body folds","A large tumour in nerve covering that may invade","A pigmented macule growing with the child","A discrete nerve sheath tumour at the surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Plexiform neurofibromas are tumours in the tissue covering nerves, occurring anywhere except the brain and spinal cord. They are large and extensive and may be locally invasive, so clinical evaluation is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has an intensely itchy eruption of small flat-topped shiny violaceous papules on both volar wrists and ankles, some coalescing into plaques with fine white surface lines. She also has lacy white streaks inside her cheeks. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen simplex chronicus","Lichen planus","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Pityriasis rosea","Psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichen planus produces intensely pruritic purple polygonal papules and plaques, classically at the ankles and volar wrists, with Wickham striae on the surface. Oral disease may show erosive lesions or the same striae. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has a raised, firm scar that developed within four weeks of a surgical incision. It remains within the original incision line and has flattened slightly over the past year. What management options apply?","choices":["Cryotherapy is always contraindicated","Excision alone, which never recurs","No treatment is ever appropriate","Systemic corticosteroids over six weeks","Intralesional steroid, sheeting, or laser"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic scar management includes intralesional injection of corticosteroid or 5-fluorouracil, compression therapy and silicone sheeting, surgical excision, and pulsed dye laser, which reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularisation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has scaly patches on the scalp with areas of hair loss and visible black dots where hairs have fractured. A potassium hydroxide preparation confirms fungal elements. His mother asks whether a medicated cream will be enough. What is the correct response?","choices":["No treatment; it resolves after puberty","An antifungal shampoo alone","A topical cream to the lesion and beyond","A topical corticosteroid first","Oral therapy; topicals miss the hair shaft"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Tinea capitis requires oral therapy; topical agents alone do not penetrate the infected hair shaft. Antifungal shampoo reduces viable spore shedding but does not replace oral treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman in her third trimester develops erythematous patches whose margins are studded with small pustules, accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and joint pains. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Impetigo","Guttate psoriasis","Generalised candidiasis","Impetigo herpetiformis","Pemphigoid gestationis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Impetigo herpetiformis is the psoriasis of pregnancy, occurring typically in the third trimester with erythematous patches whose margins are studded with subcorneal pustules. Onset is accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and arthralgia. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has Demodex-associated disease that has not responded to topical metronidazole. What topical agent is described as superior for this situation?","choices":["Azelaic acid 15 to 20 percent","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Brimonidine gel","Metronidazole 0.75 to 1 percent gel at a higher frequency","Ivermectin 1 percent cream"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Ivermectin 1 percent cream is described as superior to metronidazole for Demodex-associated papulopustular rosacea, reflecting the contribution of Demodex folliculorum mite overgrowth to the pathogenesis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Pyogenic granuloma","Dermatofibroma","Cherry angioma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired benign vascular tumour arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, most often on the head, neck, and fingers, and it bleeds readily. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated boy has a generalised itchy eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all visible at the same time. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hand, foot and mouth disease","Scabies","Impetigo","Herpes zoster","Varicella"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing, with macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts appearing simultaneously and lesions concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft, skin-coloured pedunculated papules on the neck and in the axillae, ranging from 2 to 6 mm. She asks what they are. What education is appropriate?","choices":["A marker of internal malignancy","They resolve on their own","Harmless growths where skin rubs together","Premalignant lesions requiring excision","Viral warts spread by contact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Skin tags are fibroepithelial pedunculated papillomas that are harmless growths of normal skin, forming where skin rubs together such as the armpit, neck, under the breasts, and groin. They are very common, present in a large majority by age 70, and more frequent in women and in obesity. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 3-day-old newborn has pink irregular macules on the nape of the neck and the glabella that become more noticeable when he cries. What is this lesion?","choices":["A cherry angioma","An infantile hemangioma","A pyogenic granuloma","A port-wine stain","Nevus simplex"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Nevus simplex is a congenital vascular lesion, a variant of nevus flammeus involving more superficial dermal capillaries. It is most common on the head and neck, presents as pink to erythematous irregular macules or patches, and becomes more noticeable when the infant cries. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a rapidly expanding painful ulcer with undermined violaceous borders, and repeated wound cultures are negative. What is the most accurate description of how pyoderma gangrenosum is diagnosed?","choices":["Confirmed by inflammatory bowel disease","Confirmed by a pathergy test","A diagnosis of exclusion","Confirmed by a specific biopsy finding","Confirmed by wound culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is explicitly a diagnosis of exclusion. Infectious ulcers, which are culture negative in pyoderma gangrenosum, and vascular insufficiency ulcers assessed by ankle-brachial index must be ruled out. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 62-year-old fair-skinned man who always burns and never tans has a solitary, smooth, shiny, dome-shaped red papule with a central keratin plug on his forearm that appeared and grew rapidly over seven weeks. What is the only reliable way to make the diagnosis, and why does it matter?","choices":["Biopsy","Clinical appearance alone","Observation for 3 to 6 months","A potassium hydroxide preparation","Dermoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is the only reliable method to make the diagnosis. Squamous cell carcinoma heads the differential, and the standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour precisely because of possible malignancy. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl reports excessive sweating of both palms, soles, and axillae since early adolescence. She reports no sweating during sleep. Her father had the same problem. Thyroid studies and glucose are normal. What does this pattern indicate, and what is the appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Secondary; endocrine investigation","Anxiety disorder; psychological therapy","Primary focal; topical aluminium chloride","Primary focal; botulinum toxin A","Frey syndrome; topical glycopyrronium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absence of nocturnal sweating and symmetry favour primary hyperhidrosis, which begins in adolescence, is bilateral and focal, and often follows an autosomal dominant family pattern. Aluminium chloride 20 percent applied nightly to dry skin is first-line topical therapy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with recurrent pruritic scaling of the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, and external ears. Examination demonstrates greasy, yellow-white scale over mildly erythematous, poorly demarcated patches. He reports the itching is worse when he sweats. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Topical mupirocin","Oral terbinafine","Oral doxycycline","Ketoconazole shampoo and cream","A high-potency topical corticosteroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis is caused by overgrowth of Malassezia, a normal skin flora organism, so topical antifungals are the mainstay. Antifungal shampoos such as ketoconazole and selenium sulfide are used on the scalp and antifungal creams or lotions on the face. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no symptoms attends for a routine health check, while a 55-year-old woman attends the same clinic with a specific complaint requiring evaluation. How does screening testing differ in purpose from diagnostic testing?","choices":["Screening confirms a diagnosis; diagnosis seeks occult disease","Diagnostic testing replaces the examination","Screening seeks occult disease; diagnosis reduces uncertainty","Screening is only for emergency medicine","They are interchangeable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening testing identifies the likelihood of occult disease. Diagnostic testing complements the history and physical examination, reduces uncertainty about diagnosis or prognosis, and helps decide management. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has recurrent crops of intensely itchy 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of her fingers and palms. They persist for several weeks, dry up, and then peel. Between episodes her hands are dry but clear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Bullous pemphigoid","Tinea manuum","Irritant contact dermatitis","Scabies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema produces intensely pruritic deep-seated vesicles on the palms, lateral fingers, and soles, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They persist for weeks, desiccate, and resolve with desquamation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man is stung by a wasp and within minutes develops widespread urticaria, wheezing, and hypotension. What is the immediate treatment?","choices":["A topical corticosteroid","Intramuscular or subcutaneous epinephrine","Cleaning, ice, and local anaesthetic","Local wound debridement","An oral antihistamine at home"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For an anaphylactic reaction to a hymenoptera sting the treatment is subcutaneous or intramuscular epinephrine with emergency department transfer and supportive measures. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with plaque psoriasis reports painful, stiff finger joints. He describes more than thirty minutes of morning stiffness that improves as he uses his hands during the day. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire second and third digits of the right hand. What additional finding is most characteristic of the associated condition?","choices":["Human leukocyte antigen Cw6","A positive rheumatoid factor","Symmetric small joints, sparing distal","Human leukocyte antigen B27","Stiffness worsening with activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Psoriatic arthritis is associated with human leukocyte antigen B27. The presentation described — psoriasis with joint pains frequently of the hands, stiffness lasting more than thirty minutes that is relieved with activity, and sausage digits — is the classic picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is referred with a red, warm, tender left lower leg. He has bilateral chronic ankle swelling and brown discoloration of both gaiter regions, but the left leg has become acutely more swollen and painful over two days. He is afebrile. What consideration should most urgently shape the evaluation?","choices":["Bilateral change makes cellulitis likely","Brown discoloration means infection","No fever excludes a vascular event","Start compression immediately","Acute unilateral swelling suggests thrombosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The differential for stasis dermatitis explicitly flags that acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis. Chronic bilateral changes with a new unilateral acute change is precisely that pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman develops an itchy eruption of 2 to 4 mm erythematous papules across the upper chest and forearms a few hours after her first sunny weekend of spring. Her face, exposed year-round, is unaffected. She takes no medication. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Drug-induced phototoxicity","Phytophotodermatitis","Sunburn","Polymorphous light eruption"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphous light eruption is the commonest idiopathic photodermatosis, appearing 30 minutes to hours after ultraviolet exposure in the first sunny days of spring, with papules on the décolletage and forearms and sparing of habitually exposed skin. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has an annular eruption of the forearms and the clinician is distinguishing erythema multiforme from urticaria. What feature most reliably separates them?","choices":["Only erythema multiforme itches","Wheals migrate and last under 24 hours","Only urticaria affects the limbs","Only erythema multiforme blanches","Wheals stay fixed for several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The differential states that urticarial wheals are pruritic, migratory, and blanch fully, with no fixed target lesions and individual lesions lasting less than 24 hours. Fixity over days is what identifies erythema multiforme. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is treated for scabies and asks what she should do with her sheets and clothing. What environmental measure is specified for bedding and clothing?","choices":["Bag for 24 hours","Wash in cold water","Wash, or bag for 14 days","Discard all bedding and clothing","No environmental measures needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bedding and clothing should be washed or set aside for 14 days in a plastic bag in a warm area, and high heat at 60 degrees Celsius is needed to kill mites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman and her 62-year-old husband, who takes an immunosuppressant, both ask about shingles vaccination. Who should receive the recombinant zoster vaccine, and at what interval?","choices":["A single dose from age 50","Two doses in childhood","Two doses only after an episode of zoster","Two doses from age 50, or 19 if immunosuppressed","Two doses from age 65 only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine are given to immunocompetent adults aged 50 years and over, and two doses to adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed, with a standard interval of 2 to 6 months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, light-brown papules across her forehead and dorsal hands. She reports they seemed to multiply along the line where she shaves her legs. What form is this, and what advice follows?","choices":["Flat warts; observe, as they resolve spontaneously","Plantar warts; salicylic acid and cryotherapy","Seborrheic keratoses; no treatment","Verruca vulgaris; salicylic acid to each","Molluscum contagiosum; cantharidin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flat warts are multiple smooth, slightly elevated, flat-topped, skin-coloured to light-brown papules common on the face, forehead, dorsal hands, and shins, and shaving can spread lesions through autoinoculation. Observation is reasonable because spontaneous resolution is likely. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 143"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with cellulitis of the leg has an area that is tense, cyanotic, and bronzed with blanching. What is the significance of this finding?","choices":["Increase the dose and observe","A drug reaction to the antibiotic","Deep vein thrombosis; anticoagulate","The expected look of resolving cellulitis","Devitalised tissue; antibiotics cannot reach it"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Devitalised tissue that is tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed, and blanched will not be perfused, so antibiotics will not get to the site. Surgical debridement is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with red hair has multiple small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across her nose and cheeks. Her mother reports they darken every summer and fade in winter. What is the inheritance pattern, and what treatment is specifically not used?","choices":["Autosomal dominant; one treatment clears them","Autosomal recessive; cryotherapy is first line","Autosomal dominant; cryotherapy is not used","X-linked; response is equal in all skin types","Somatic mutation; excision is first line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ephelides, or freckles, are autosomal dominant and appear most commonly on sun-exposed skin of fair-skinned individuals, often with blonde or red hair. They become more pronounced in spring and summer and fade in winter. Cryotherapy is not used because it is difficult given the size of the lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 50-year-old labourer has a thickened, dry, scaly right palm that he has attributed to hard physical work. He also has scaling on both soles. What is the likely diagnosis, and how does it typically present?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Tinea manuum","Chronic paronychia","Irritant contact dermatitis","Palmar psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tinea manuum is associated with tinea pedis, and both palms and soles can be infected at the same time. Patients are often unaware of the infection, believing the changes are due to dry skin or hard physical labour, and the involved palm is thickened, dry, and scaly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 13-year-old boy has several elevated round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface on the fingers. Paring one reveals tiny red-black dots. Skin lines are interrupted where the lesions sit. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Clavus","Molluscum contagiosum","Verruca vulgaris","Seborrheic keratosis","Callus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Common warts are elevated round papules under 1 cm with a rough greyish surface, most often on the hands and fingers, and tiny red or black dots representing thrombosed dilated capillaries become more prominent when the surface is trimmed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 142"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man is found to have hundreds of small papules over the trunk and extremities consistent with generalised granuloma annulare. What screening is most appropriate?","choices":["No screening is indicated","Immunoglobulin A tissue transglutaminase","Chest radiograph","Colonoscopy","Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Fasting glucose and haemoglobin A1c are used to screen for diabetes in generalised granuloma annulare, alongside a lipid panel and thyroid studies for associated systemic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has multiple small flesh-coloured papules scattered over the trunk, and the clinician examines each one closely. What is the characteristic morphological feature of a molluscum contagiosum lesion?","choices":["Central umbilication of a pearly dome-shaped papule","A stuck-on look with comedone-like openings","A rough grey surface with thrombosed capillaries","Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base","An annular plaque with central clearing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lesions are discrete, smooth-surfaced, firm, flesh-coloured, dome-shaped pearly papules averaging 3 to 5 mm, and central umbilication is characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has recurrent grouped bites appearing each morning after sleeping in her own bed. What is essential in the management of a bedbug infestation beyond treating the patient's skin?","choices":["Treating all contacts with permethrin","Washing bedding at 60 degrees Celsius alone","A professional exterminator","No environmental action","A pediculicide to the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of the patient is symptomatic with local wound care, topical antiseptic or antibiotic cream for secondary infection, and topical corticosteroids or oral antihistamines for pruritus. A professional exterminator is necessary to eradicate the infestation itself. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has intensely itchy finger-web lesions and a scraping is taken from a burrow. What is the definitive method of diagnosing scabies, and what dermoscopic finding supports it?","choices":["A burrow ink test alone","Microscopy plus a positive Nikolsky sign","Potassium hydroxide showing spaghetti and meatballs","Serology for anti-mite antibodies","Microscopy of the mite, ova, or feces"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Definitive diagnosis is by microscopic identification of the organism, ova, or feces, obtained by skin scraping. The classic dermoscopic finding is the delta-wing jet sign, representing the dense scabies head, body, eggs, and burrow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man whose brother died of melanoma asks how to monitor his own skin between annual clinic visits. What should patients be taught about melanoma self-examination?","choices":["Monthly, including scalp, palms, soles, nails","Annually, limited to sun-exposed skin","Not needed if a clinician examines yearly","Report only lesions over 6 mm","Focus on counting total naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should perform monthly self-examination using ABCDE and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles, and nails, with prompt assessment of evolution, bleeding, or ulceration. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man in Tennessee has a painful lesion on the thigh two days after cleaning out a closet. Examination shows a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin, and beyond that a large area of erythema. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema migrans","Cellulitis","Hobo spider bite","Black widow spider bite","Brown recluse spider bite"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The hallmark of a brown recluse bite is the red, white, and blue sign — a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin surrounded by a large erythematous area. Loxosceles reclusa is abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast and shelters in undisturbed places. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man presents with vesicles in the right ophthalmic division distribution, including lesions on the tip of his nose. What does that finding signify, and what should be done?","choices":["Hutchinson sign; start antivirals immediately","Hutchinson sign; its absence excludes eye disease","Disseminated zoster","Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Zoster sine herpete"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus involves the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. Hutchinson sign — lesions on the tip or side of the nose — increases ocular risk, but its absence does not exclude eye involvement, and systemic antiviral therapy should be started immediately. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with an itchy rash on her forearm. She had a laceration dressed with an adhesive bandage four days ago. Examination shows a sharply demarcated rectangular erythematous plaque with tiny vesicles corresponding exactly to the adhesive portions of the dressing, and a rectangle of normal skin in the centre where the cotton pad had lain. The laceration itself is clean and healing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis to the adhesive","Herpes simplex virus reactivation","Irritant contact dermatitis from the wound cleanser","Wound infection of the underlying laceration","Cellulitis of the forearm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients allergic to the adhesive in an adhesive bandage present with a bandage-shaped rash that spares the middle area where the cotton lay. The eruption reproduces the shape of the contactant, which is the signature of allergic contact dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has maceration and erythematous erosions between the third and fourth toes with associated fissuring. What form of tinea pedis is this, and is it the commonest?","choices":["Candidal intertrigo of the toe webs","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Hyperkeratotic tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the least common form"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Interdigital tinea pedis is the most common form, caused by Trichophyton species, producing maceration and erythematous erosions or scales between the toes, especially in the third and fourth interspaces, with associated fissures. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy and his mother, who teaches at his school, both present with honey-crusted perioral lesions. In which patient with impetigo should a culture be obtained rather than relying on clinical appearance alone?","choices":["Any child under five","Any patient with honey-coloured crusting","A healthcare worker or teacher","Any patient on topical mupirocin","Only those who failed oral antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained if the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection, with healthcare workers and teachers given as the examples, or if acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a concern. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a keratotic lesion on the ear that has enlarged over six weeks and now bleeds when he shaves. Which findings in a keratotic lesion are red flags for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Colour variegation with border irregularity","Sandpaper texture and small size","A pearly translucent surface with telangiectasias","Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration","A stuck-on appearance with comedone-like openings"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Red flags for squamous cell carcinoma include rapid growth, pain, bleeding, and ulceration, and the same features in an actinic keratosis prompt biopsy to exclude early carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman is told she has multiple actinic keratoses and asks whether that means she has skin cancer. What is the relationship between actinic keratosis and keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["It progresses to basal cell carcinoma","It progresses to melanoma","Invasive carcinoma from the outset","Entirely benign and unrelated","Premalignant, on a continuum with carcinoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin. Actinic keratosis is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a blotchy eruption on the trunk whose edges cannot be traced, and the clinician documents it as poorly demarcated. What does this term convey?","choices":["Clear borders but irregular internal colour","It changes when pressure is applied","Smaller than 1 cm","Irregular, blotchy, poorly defined borders","Elevated above the surrounding skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A poorly demarcated lesion has an irregular or blotchy appearance without well-defined borders, in contrast to a well-demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man is admitted with extensive flaccid bullae and painful erosions involving the mouth, chest, and groin. Biopsy demonstrates acantholysis and immunofluorescence confirms pathogenic antibodies. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Oral doxycycline with nicotinamide","Ultrapotent topical corticosteroids alone","Urgent treatment with rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone","Dapsone as single-agent therapy","Low-dose methotrexate with folic acid supplementation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is a life-threatening blistering disorder requiring urgent treatment, initially rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone. When steroids are used they are usually paired with a steroid-sparing agent such as azathioprine or mycophenolate so the patient can eventually be transitioned off. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has multiple tense, clear fluid-filled lesions on the trunk, the largest measuring 1.8 cm. What is the correct term for the largest lesion?","choices":["Pustule","Wheal","Nodule","Vesicle","Bulla"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A bulla is a fluid-filled lesion greater than 1 cm. At 1.8 cm this lesion is above the threshold that separates it from a vesicle. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a new pigmented longitudinal band in the nail of one thumb that has widened over six months and is broader proximally than distally. Pigment extends onto the proximal nail fold. What is the concern, and what is the sign at the nail fold called?","choices":["Benign longitudinal melanonychia","Nail unit melanoma; Hutchinson sign","Glomus tumour","Subungual haematoma","Onychomycosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New or evolving longitudinal melanonychia in one digit with increasing width, irregular colour or spacing, and proximal widening or a triangular shape raises nail unit melanoma. Pigment on the periungual skin is Hutchinson sign, and the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with a rash on her trunk. Ten days before the current eruption she noticed a single oval, sharply demarcated salmon-coloured plaque about 4 cm across on her upper back that became scaly and began to clear in the centre. Now numerous smaller similar lesions have appeared over the trunk, with their long axes aligned along the lines of the ribs. She feels well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Guttate psoriasis","Secondary syphilis","Pityriasis rosea","Nummular eczema","Tinea corporis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A herald patch occurs in 50 to 90 percent of cases, preceding the exanthem: a single 2 to 5 cm round or oval, sharply demarcated pink or salmon-coloured plaque on the chest, neck, or back that clears centrally, leaving a collarette of scale. The eruption of smaller similar lesions follows 1 to 2 weeks later. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger, with a longitudinal groove in the adjacent nail. What is the nature of this lesion?","choices":["A collection of lipid-laden macrophages","An eccrine duct neoplasm","A true cyst lined by epithelium","A pseudo-cyst of mucin extruded from the joint","An overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digital mucous cysts are pseudo-cysts without a cellular lining or true capsule. They represent extrusion of mucinous contents from a local joint space into the surrounding dermis, typically over the distal interphalangeal joint, and may distort the nail. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has an abrupt eruption of small pustules on the thighs and buttocks after wearing occlusive workout clothing. Each pustule sits on an erythematous base and is pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Folliculitis","Pseudofolliculitis barbae","Miliaria","Furunculosis","Acne vulgaris"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Folliculitis is inflammation within the wall and ostia of the hair follicle, producing small papules and pustules on an erythematous base pierced by a central hair, most often from Staphylococcus aureus. Diabetes and occlusive clothing are listed risk factors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has thick scaly plaques on both elbows and knees present for years. Examination shows well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale, also involving the sacrum and scalp. Lifting the scale produces pinpoint bleeding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis","Lichen planus","Seborrheic dermatitis","Nummular eczema","Psoriasis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Plaque psoriasis produces erythematous to salmon-pink plaques with silvery scale on extensor elbows and knees, trunk, scalp, umbilicus, and sacrum, and Auspitz sign is pinpoint bleeding after scale removal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 2-week-old neonate develops blisters at sites of handling and diapering, and genetic testing is planned. Which subtype carries the greatest long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma and requires the most intensive multidisciplinary surveillance?","choices":["Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","Kindler syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Acquired epidermolysis bullosa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa causes severe scarring, and the multidisciplinary team includes haematology and oncology specifically for squamous cell carcinoma surveillance. Scarring subtypes carry the malignancy risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has central facial papules and pustules, and the clinician looks for the one lesion type that would settle whether this is acne. What is the hallmark lesion of acne vulgaris, and why does it matter diagnostically?","choices":["The telangiectasia, whose presence confirms acne","The nodule, whose presence defines the diagnosis","The pustule, whose absence argues against acne","The comedone, whose absence argues against acne and toward rosacea","The papule, which is unique to acne among facial eruptions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Comedones are the hallmark of acne vulgaris, and the differential specifically distinguishes rosacea by papules and pustules in the middle third of the face with telangiectasias, flushing, and an absence of comedones. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man who keeps a pet tarantula presents with itching of both forearms and a red, irritated eye. What cutaneous and ocular effects can tarantulas cause?","choices":["No cutaneous or ocular effects","Necrotic ulceration from cytotoxic venom","A serpentine advancing track","Muscle spasm and abdominal pain","Embedded hairs; conjunctivitis or granuloma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Tarantula hairs may land and embed on skin or eyes when the animal is threatened, producing cutaneous responses ranging from mild local pruritus to granulomatous reactions, and conjunctivitis or corneal granuloma in the eye. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a slow-growing 1 cm papule on the nose with a pearly translucent quality and visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched. There is central erosion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Molluscum contagiosum","Basal cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nodular basal cell carcinoma is a papule or nodule with central erosion growing slowly over years, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by stretching the skin. It is the most common form of cancer. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl with red hair has multiple freckles across the nose and cheeks, and her mother asks what can be done about them. What is the cornerstone of management for ephelides?","choices":["Surgical excision of each macule","Cryotherapy to each lesion","Systemic depigmenting therapy","No management is possible","Sun protection with patient education"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sun protection is the cornerstone of management for ephelides, with proper patient education and counselling described as key. Topical depigmenting agents such as hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids, and botanicals may be used, and intense pulsed light is an option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 42-year-old Black woman has multiple small, smooth, firm dark brown papules 1 to 3 mm on the face and neck. What is the diagnosis, and how does it relate to seborrheic keratosis?","choices":["Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, a premalignant variant","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans, a benign variant","Melanocytic naevi","Syringomas","Multiple lentigines"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dermatosis papulosa nigrans consists of multiple small, smooth, firm black or dark brown papules 1 to 5 mm on the face and neck, identical to small seborrheic keratoses. It is thought to be genetic and is believed to be a developmental defect of the hair follicle. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with thickened, discoloured toenails is having a specimen taken to confirm onychomycosis before oral therapy is started. From where should the specimen be taken?","choices":["The surrounding proximal nail fold","Any nail on the affected foot, whether or not it appears diseased","A plucked hair from the affected limb","The most proximal accessible diseased nail bed or subungual debris","The distal free edge of the nail plate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sampling should be from the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed and subungual debris, where viable organism is concentrated, using potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl had impetigo three weeks ago. She now presents with facial oedema, tea-coloured urine, and hypertension. Her mother asks whether the antibiotics failed. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis; earlier antibiotics would have","Treatment failure needing a new antibiotic","An allergic reaction to the antibiotic","Rheumatic fever after the skin infection","Glomerulonephritis; antibiotics do not prevent it"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis may follow impetigo, especially in 3 to 7 year olds, and is characterised by sudden oedema, haematuria, and hypertension. Antibiotics do not prevent it because activation of the immune response most often precedes antibiotic treatment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"An 18-year-old boy is prescribed a topical antibiotic for acne, and benzoyl peroxide is added to the regimen. Why is benzoyl peroxide recommended alongside topical or oral antibiotic therapy for acne?","choices":["It decreases sebum excretion","It suppresses Cutibacterium and limits resistance","It is comedolytic and normalises keratinisation","It provides ultraviolet protection","It inhibits tyrosinase and lightens pigment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Benzoyl peroxide is keratolytic and anti-inflammatory and suppresses Cutibacterium acnes. It is recommended alongside antimicrobials specifically to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance, and combination products exist for that purpose. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A student proposes a rare diagnosis to explain a very typical presentation in a 30-year-old woman. What does the principle 'place your bets on uncommon manifestations of common conditions rather than common manifestations of uncommon conditions' mean in practice?","choices":["Assume the most serious diagnosis first","An atypical presentation of a common disease is likelier","A typical presentation of a rare disease is likelier","Rare diagnoses should never be considered","All diagnoses are equally likely until tested"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Common things occur commonly — when you hear hoof beats think of horses, not zebras. An unusual presentation of a common condition is more probable than a textbook presentation of something rare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has several uniformly tan-brown macules on the dorsal hands and face that have appeared gradually over years. One lesion is larger, asymmetric, and has irregular borders with darker areas within it. What should be done about that lesion?","choices":["Evaluate it with dermoscopy for lentigo maligna","Treat it as a seborrheic keratosis and leave it alone","Apply a topical retinoid and review in six months","Treat it as a café-au-lait macule and observe","Reassure her that it is a solar lentigo like the others"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lentigo maligna, which is melanoma in situ, is asymmetric with irregular border and colour and darker areas, and dermoscopy is described as essential. A solar lentigo that has diverged from its uniform neighbours requires that evaluation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man presents in late summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules 4 to 5 mm over the upper back and shoulders, some coalescing. They did not tan with the rest of his skin. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Postinflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Vitiligo","Pityriasis versicolor","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis versicolor is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia and produces velvety tan, pink, or white scaling macules 4 to 5 mm on the neck, upper arms, trunk, and groin. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 78"}]};
